# •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
# 📊 NTOPNG — ENVIRONMENT (EXAMPLE) 📊
# •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••

# Copy to .env alongside compose.yaml, then edit.
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# host-setup.sh does this for you on first run, then stops so you can edit
# before anything starts.
#
# One package, two network positions. POSITION is the first thing to set; it
# selects the netplan, changes what LOCAL_NETS means, and changes how strict
# the script is about WEB_BIND. Every value below is annotated for both.
#
# Values are sized for a 32 GB host. Total container ceilings come to ~16 GB,
# leaving ~16 GB for kernel and NIC buffers. That headroom is intentional —
# do not spend it.

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                       🧭 POSITION
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# ext  Outside the firewall, inline between the ISP handoff and the firewall's
#      WAN interface. bridge0 is addressless; you reach the box on a separate
#      management NIC. WEB_BIND may never be a wildcard.
#
# int  Inside the firewall, inline on a LAN link. bridge0 takes a DHCP lease,
#      so the box is reachable on the segment it monitors and needs no second
#      NIC. WEB_BIND may be a wildcard, with a warning.
#
# Changing this after deployment changes which netplan is installed, which is
# a network reconfiguration — host-setup.sh will ask before applying it.
POSITION=ext

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                        📡 CAPTURE
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# The bridge master device ntopng monitors. This is the ONLY interface it sees.
# Same for both positions.
MON_IF=bridge0

# Physical member ports of the bridge, space separated. Used by the netprep
# one-shot, and by host-setup.sh for ring buffers, offload checks and the udev
# re-trigger. These names must match the netplan file for your POSITION:
#   ext -> etc/netplan/10-ntopng-bridge-ext.yaml
#   int -> etc/netplan/10-ntopng-bridge-int.yaml
#
# Quoted because this is the one multi-word value in the file. Compose strips
# the quotes and reads it identically either way, but unquoted it is not valid
# shell — `BRIDGE_PORTS=enp1s0 enp2s0` means "run the command enp2s0" — so the
# quotes keep the file safe to source. host-setup.sh parses rather than sources
# it and accepts both forms.
BRIDGE_PORTS="enp1s0 enp2s0"

# What ntopng treats as "inside". Comma separated CIDRs, no spaces.
#
# THIS IS THE SETTING THAT DIFFERS MOST BETWEEN THE TWO POSITIONS, and getting
# it wrong produces a sensor that runs perfectly and tells you nothing.
#
# POSITION=ext — use the WAN prefix, NOT your LAN.
#   bridge0 carries the link between the ISP and the firewall's WAN interface.
#   The firewall NATs, or this box would not be "outside" — so no RFC1918
#   address ever appears on that wire. Setting your inside ranges here
#   classifies every host as remote, leaves the Local Hosts view empty, and
#   makes --dns-mode=1 resolve nothing. Use the prefix the ISP hands you; a
#   single static address is a /32.
#     LOCAL_NETS=203.0.113.0/29
#
# POSITION=int — use your actual LAN ranges, all of them.
#   This is the pre-NAT side, so RFC1918 is exactly what you will see. List
#   every internal subnet that can appear on this wire, including VLANs
#   trunked across it, or those hosts land in Remote Hosts and their names
#   never resolve.
#     LOCAL_NETS=192.168.1.0/24,192.168.20.0/24,10.10.0.0/16
#
# host-setup.sh warns when this looks wrong for the position — RFC1918 under
# ext, or no RFC1918 at all under int. It warns rather than refuses, because
# either can be legitimate on an unusual link.
LOCAL_NETS=203.0.113.0/29

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                         🌐 WEB UI
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# The address the ntopng UI listens on. ntopng ships admin/admin, so read the
# paragraph for your position before changing this.
#
# POSITION=ext — 127.0.0.1 or the management NIC address. NOTHING ELSE.
#   This host is on the dirty side of the firewall. Bind the MANAGEMENT NIC
#   address — the static, gateway-less interface configured by its own netplan
#   file (see README, "Management interface"). 127.0.0.1 plus an SSH tunnel is
#   the safe fallback and the default here. host-setup.sh REFUSES to start the
#   stack if this is 0.0.0.0, :: or *.
#     WEB_BIND=10.9.9.10        # example: the management NIC address
#
# POSITION=int — 0.0.0.0 is permitted, and is usually what you want.
#   bridge0's address comes from DHCP, so there is no stable address to pin
#   here, and `optional: true` on the bridge means the stack can start before
#   the lease lands — a pinned address would fail to bind on a cold boot. A
#   wildcard binds regardless and you reach the UI by hostname:
#       http://<hostname>.local:3000/
#   host-setup.sh warns and asks for confirmation rather than refusing.
#
#   Tighter alternative: make a DHCP reservation (against bridge0's own MAC —
#   see the netplan file) and pin that address here. You lose the cold-boot
#   robustness above; the boot unit retries, so in practice this is survivable.
#
#   Either way, CHANGE THE admin/admin PASSWORD AT FIRST LOGIN. A LAN is not a
#   trusted network, and this UI can see every conversation on the segment.
WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1
WEB_PORT=3000

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                  🧮 NTOPNG SIZING
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# MAX_FLOWS / MAX_HOSTS are the knobs that actually consume RAM. NTOPNG_MEM is
# only the ceiling. Raising NTOPNG_MEM alone changes nothing.
#
# These are a deliberately conservative bump from the 1000000/250000 that fitted
# an 8 GB ceiling, taking some of the 8 GB that ClickHouse used to reserve.
# Treat them as a starting point, not an answer: watch the ntopng log for
# "too many flows" / "too many hosts" and the System > Health page for table
# occupancy, then size from a week of real traffic.
#
# Position note: an int sensor typically sees MORE hosts and more short-lived
# flows than an ext one, because it sees pre-NAT internal chatter — broadcast,
# mDNS, SMB, backup traffic — that never crosses the firewall. If you retune
# for a LAN, MAX_HOSTS is usually the one that binds first.
TZ=Etc/UTC
MAX_FLOWS=1500000
MAX_HOSTS=400000
NTOPNG_MEM=12g

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                          💾 REDIS
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# Policy is noeviction, so hitting REDIS_MAXMEM breaks ntopng rather than
# degrading it. REDIS_MEM must stay well above REDIS_MAXMEM to survive the
# copy-on-write spike during RDB snapshots — a fork can transiently push RSS
# toward 2x the dataset, and memswap_limit == mem_limit means no swap absorbs
# it. 2gb against a 4g ceiling is that 2x; 3gb against 4g was not.
REDIS_MAXMEM=2gb
REDIS_MEM=4g

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                   📜 FLOW HISTORY
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# None, deliberately, at either position. ntopng's --dump-flows to ClickHouse
# (and Kafka, and the ClickHouse cluster/cloud variants) is an Enterprise
# M/L/XL/XXL feature. With no /etc/ntopng.license the daemon logs
#     [LICENSE] ntopng will start in community mode
# and ignores the flag — so a ClickHouse container here would reserve 8 GB to
# receive nothing. Live flows, the host table, alerting and the UI all work in
# community mode; only historical flow storage is missing.
#
# If you buy a licence: mount it at /etc/ntopng.license, add --dump-flows back
# with --fail-invalid-license, and restore the clickhouse service.

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
